## Approach

- Reuse over rebuild: the gate consults `check-reviewers.sh`, which already owns the probe,
  stamp cache, serial lock, and auth-retry logic. The first launch against a degraded tool pays
  one bounded probe; `check-reviewers.sh` writes a fresh `false` stamp, so all subsequent
  launches and `launch-review.sh` retry cycles within the 60s TTL fast-fail instantly. Worst-case
  added latency is ~one probe per 60s window instead of one 20-30 min hang.
- Activation polarity (Decision 2): the gate is OFF at the script level unless
  `LARCH_EXTERNAL_HEALTH_CHECK_TIMEOUT` is a positive integer. Production turns it on through the
  session-env writers (`30` default). This delivers "on in production, off in test/CI" with zero
  test-harness churn — tests never set the var and never source the session-env writers.
  - Considered alternative: default-on inside `run-external-agent.sh` with a `=0` opt-out. Rejected
    for L1 because every stub harness that drives `run-external-agent.sh --tool codex|cursor`
    (test-run-external-agent, test-launch-review, test-step2-dispatch, the implementer harnesses,
    etc.) would fast-fail in CI where no real binary exists, forcing an opt-out edit in each.
    Reviewers may revisit if broader always-on coverage is preferred over session-env coverage.
- Waterfall integration without classifier changes: codex `7` / cursor `8` + empty output is the
  existing `health`/`health-probe` path. CI family falls through (health is not `other`, so
  `run_waterfall` does not short-circuit); review family retries (cheaply, cached) then falls
  through. Both reach Claude.

## Edge cases

- Gate enabled but `timeout`/`gtimeout` absent (macOS Bash 3.2 without coreutils): run
  `check-reviewers.sh` unwrapped; its internal `LARCH_PROBE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` still bounds the
  probe. Gate stays bounded.
- Fresh `false` stamp from session start: gate fast-fails instantly with no new probe.
- Fresh `true` stamp: gate proceeds with no new probe (the common healthy path adds ~0 latency).
- `--tool` other than codex/cursor (e.g. claude): gate is a no-op.
- Parallel panel launches: `check-reviewers.sh` already uses atomic stamp writes + a Darwin
  serial lock, so concurrent gate calls are safe.
- Gate-infra failure (missing/broken `check-reviewers.sh`): fail OPEN (proceed), never block a
  launch because the gate broke.

## Failure modes

1. False-positive on a healthy-but-slow tool (transient network slows the probe past the bound) →
   needless Claude fallback. Earliest signal: a rise in `health-probe fast-fail` diag lines /
   Claude-tier usage. Mitigation: generous `30s` default; the `false` stamp expires in 60s so the
   next window can recover; `=0` opt-out.
2. Accidental gate activation in tests/CI → stub launches fast-fail. Earliest signal: harness
   failures citing exit `7`/`8` or `health-probe fast-fail`. Mitigation: gate OFF unless the env
   var is set; production-only via session-env writers; gate tests stub `check-reviewers.sh`.
3. Probe cost/latency creep (a small `Respond with OK` call per 60s window) → marginal token
   spend. Earliest signal: extra codex/cursor probe rows in the token ledger. Mitigation: stamp
   cache (≤1 probe / 60s), `LARCH_EXTERNAL_AUTH_RETRIES=1` for the gate, internal probe-timeout
   bound.

